schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: "Aragon's 2023 treasury dissolution shows unilateral structural moves can break DAO trust even when 'winning'"
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    In 2023, investors bought up ANT tokens to force a payout of Aragon's
    ~$200M treasury, which Aragon's team called a "51% attack" by "risk-free-
    value raiders." Aragon cancelled the handover, dissolved its association,
    and redeemed the treasury (~86,000 ETH, ~$155M) without a community vote;
    the remaining DAO then voted to fund legal action against its own founders.
  evidence: >-
    Cited to CoinDesk ("Aragon Cancels Planned Community Control of $200M
    Treasury...") and Cointelegraph ("Aragon Association Dissolves, Disburses
    $155M...") reporting from 2023.
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/conflict-resolution.md
  interpretation: >-
    Used as the article's central warning that reaching for a unilateral
    structural lever (even in response to a genuine attack) to end a conflict
    tends to detonate trust rather than resolve it — "no clever mechanism
    saves you once trust is gone."
  uncertainty: >-
    Figures (ETH amount, USD values) reflect values reported at the time in
    the cited press sources and will not match current ETH price; "51% attack"
    and "risk-free-value raiders" are Aragon's own framing of the investor
    bloc, a contested characterization the article does not independently verify.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/conflict-resolution.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  work_order: wo-ee6204d14f2b
